        textProvided: "The Kilns, Headington Quarry, Oxford<br>17/3/58<br><br>Dear Shephard—It is perhaps almost an advantage that I came to your introduction knowing almost nothing about this text. This enabled me to gauge how clear you were making things—for of course all expositions seem easy if we start by knowing what is expounded. Frankly, I don’t think your pp. 2-6 are as lucid as they ought to be and can, no doubt, be made by re-writing. First of all one is started on a false trail by version. Then comes the absolute mystery at the bottom of p. 5. “Agreement between MSS” usually means agreement in readings between MSS of the same work. “Agreement in content” doesn’t clear matters up. No one expected them to agree in shape or size? Nor is the student given any hint why SK, SM etc. are being mentioned at all. Could you not use a good many more words making clear what you are going to tabulate and <i>why</i> before you go on to the tabulation? And wd. the earlier part run more easily in the order<br><br>1. This work exists in English, French and Latin.<br>2. Descriptions of the English MSS<br>3. ——————————  French MSS	if, and so far as, necessary [in the MSS this note is applied both to this and to the next numbered item by an arrow or brace]<br>4. ——————————  Latin MSS<br>5. But there is evidence that the English is the original<br>6. It will be necessary for the following reasons to mention other works<br>7. About SK, SM etc.<br>I am probably being stupid, but some of your other readers may be so too. After that, you got much easier. I enclose my notes, of very varying importance. If you revise (but you ought to let someone else vet it too) I suggest that changes shd. be all in the direction of simpler (and therefore more elegant) syntax and always the most precise or most obvious word. Often, see <i>object</i> for <i>substance</i> on p. 45 these coincide. Perhaps I should add that I have a severe cold in my head and some of my discontents may arise fm that, not fm you!<br><br>Yours<br>C.S. Lewis<br><br>P. 1 Para 1. <i>Self-inflicted pain</i>. True, but perhaps misleading. Does he not restrain rather than incite in this direction.<br><i>to us</i>, well of course. Perhaps most of your readers, however, need more to be reminded that this sort of life is still being attempted—is a perennial, not a “period,” thing.<br>Para 2 <i>new</i>. I suppose this will be gone into later?<br>P. 2. Para 1. last line. <i>various</i>. This leads one to think it was written in Latin or French and the Eng. texts are translations. Then, when in l. 4. one finds the French and Latin also called <i>versions</i> one gets puzzled. (For <i>versions</i> in l. 3 read MSS? For <i>versions</i> in l. 4, read <i>translations</i>? If that is what is meant).<br>P. 4 Para. 4. <i>texts</i>. Again, the precise sense of the word is not absolutely clear.<br>Para 5 <i>However there can</i>. I shd. prefer (a) <i>But there can</i> (b) <i>There can, however</i>,<br>Pp. 5-6. Not at all easy to follow. Oughtn’t Ste, st. etc. to have a new para. and a sentence explaining why they are being introduced? I think this whole bit shd. be re-written with the needs of a reader starting from scratch in view.<br>P. 17 <i>cannot be extended indefinitely</i>. I don’t know what this means. We have been given not an argument but a hypothesis. Do you mean “is not v. probable” or “does not cover all the facts,” or what?<br>P. 18. Para. 2. Last sentence. <i>But indeed</i>. The transition of thought implied by this is not clear to me.<br>P. 19. Para. 2. <i>far into the 13<sup>th</sup></i>. Does this mean “well within”? I understand a thing “lasting (or continuing, or penetrating) <i>far into</i> a century” but hardly “referring far into”<br>P. 21 l. 2. <i>shall</i>. Surely <i>will</i>?<br>P. 26. Para 2. <i>Curzon to become</i>. Morally??? English?<br>P. 30 ll. 4-8. Cd. we say this with fewer as’s? (probably, pirate as much as merchant”?)<br>P. 31. Para 2. Is <i>later times</i> a good antithesis to <i>month</i>?<br>P. 32. Para 2. <i>cloud of witnesses</i>. A trifle hackneyed, and anyway a recluse can be a witness only in a <i>very</i> “spiritual” sense.<br>P. 35. Para 2. <i>prescribes of</i>. A rather harsh construction. “Lays down rules for … for … and for”?<br>Last line <i>as in the case of</i>. Wouldn’t <i>like</i> be more English?<br>P. 39. Para 2. <i>scrupulously</i>. Am I pedantic in feeling that when we are writing about a theological matter we must not use this word in its popular sense? I’d feel happier with <i>strictly</i>, or <i>exactly</i>.<br>P. 43. Para 2. <i>a kind of insertion</i>. Is this v. unlike the account of Adam’s creation in <i>Genesis</i>?<br>P. 45. Para 2. <i>Puritanism</i>. If we must use the word in this sense let us at least give it inverted commas (and perhaps a footnote to say that this has v. little connection, if any, with historical Puritanism (e.g. Cartwright). We must not <i>encourage</i> misconceptions.<br>5th l. from end. <i>substance</i>. Don’t you mean <i>object</i>?<br>P. 46. Para 2. Shd. we note that Anselm is pretty close (on a far more popular level) to Boethius in this?<br>P. 55. Memo—it all occurs in Islamic mysticism too, doesn’t it? But perhaps we can let that sleeping dog lie.<br>P. 57. Para 2. l. 5. <i>Divine person</i>. As we are going on to what concerns especially the humanity of Christ, is the word <i>divine</i> here something of a Red Herring?<br>P. 58. Para 2. <i>And Bernard</i>. Wd. either <i>But Bernard</i>, or simply <i>Bernard</i> do more justice to the actual movement of your thought?<br>P. 61 l. 8. <i>correlate</i>. Is this any improvement on <i>object</i>?<br>P. 62 para 1. <i>acquired …. interchangeable</i>. I have no idea what this means.<br>P. 63 para 2. l. 10. <i>egocentric love is deepened</i>. This surely wd. naturally mean the v. opposite of what you intend, i.e. that we love ourselves simply (quantitatively) <i>more</i> & therefore love God and our neighbour less. You can’t say all you want to in so few words.<br>P. 69. para. 2. l. 9. <i>He aims beyond mere assent</i>. Wd. “he aims at something more than mere assent” be easier?<br>Last sentence. I am not quite sure what you mean. Do you mean that when people come to analyze they are puzzled by something that did not puzzle them as readers? Or that some transitions are difficult to represent in an analysis? Or that those who read analyses are puzzled?<br>P. 71. para 2. L. 2. <i>emerges from its dependence</i>. Does this mean <i>1.</i> Comes to light by reason of its dependence on or <i>2.</i> ceases to depend on?",
        type: "Letter",
        notes: null
