        id: "kzbbe9",
        title: "Letter to Geoffrey Shepherd (19 March 1960)",
        printedIn: "Steve Beebe and Joel Heck, “C.S. Lewis’s Role as General Editor of <i>Nelson’s Medieval and Renaissance Library Series</i>” in <i>SEVEN: An Anglo-American Literary Review</i>",
        issueOrVolume: "vol. 39",
        pageRange: "42-43",
        year: 2023,
        monthAndDay: null,
        textProvided: "The Kilns, Headington Quarry, Oxford<br>19 March 1960<br><br>Dear Shepherd<br><br>I’ve now read the Introduction, and the notes to FL. I like this edition v. much; the Introduction is one of the best we have had. My comments turn on smallish points, except for those on the editor’s treatment of “convention” and “topics.” Some of this might be reconsidered. At least I feel that Curtius’ tendency to consider literature a closed system which feeds solely on itself has been a misleading inference.<br><br>The notes tell us what they ought, but I think they wd. stand the pruning knife. Are you sure that our series is not overdoing the “parallel passage” business? The primary use of parallels, <i>me judice</i>, is to make clearer what is difficult in the text before us. Next comes their use as evidence that this poet was indebted to this or that predecessor. Illustrative of the general homogeneity of a whole school, I shd. put third; the Introduction makes that clear enough. But when it comes to citing parallels for “God wot” or “worth £1000,” does it not become ridiculous? When you or I are <i>using</i> the edn., aren’t the parallels the point of the notes we find least useful, and don’t they irritate us by burying in a mass of irrelevancies the things we turn to the notes to find?<br><br>I suppose I now stand by till the notes on AL arrive?<br><br>Yours<br>C.S. Lewis",
        type: "Letter",
        notes: ["Lewis was reviewing the manuscript that was to become <i>The Floure and the Leafe</i> and <i>The Assembly of Ladies</i>, edited by Derek A. Pearsall, published in 1962 as the sixth volume in Nelson’s Medieval and Renaissance Library."]
