        title: "Letter to Geoffrey Shepherd (22 March 1958)",
        printedIn: "Steve Beebe and Joel Heck, “C.S. Lewis’s Role as General Editor of <i>Nelson’s Medieval and Renaissance Library Series</i>” in <i>SEVEN: An Anglo-American Literary Review</i>",
        issueOrVolume: "vol. 39",
        pageRange: "41-42",
        year: 2023,
        monthAndDay: null,
        textProvided: "The Kilns, Headington Quarry, Oxford<br>22/3/58<br><br>Dear Shepherd—I must have expressed myself badly if you think I condemned your “manner of writing” as “irritating”. I objected in one place not to your manner but to your <i>ordonnance</i> as needlessly difficult. I object to “substance” (of a desire or hope—I forget which it was) because I don’t know what it means. Is it the thing hoped for or desired? if so, surely more people wd. understand “object”? Is it the “substance” of this hope or desire as distinct from its accidents? If so—<i>tantamne rem tam negligenter!</i> We’d need a lot more help. If it means neither of these, then I just don’t understand it at all. Others may. There is no need to take my decision as final.<br><br>I don’t, myself, want the introduction made shorter. If the publisher absolutely insists then your only two courses are<br><br>a. To go through saving a sentence and even a word here and there. I have found it is wonderful how many words one <i>can</i> save that way (what a critique this is on my own style!) but of course there is great danger of obscurity—as Horace taught us!<br>b. To jettison matter. I suppose some of the theology wd. have to be sacrificed.<br>c. Possibly the philological part could be made more purely tabular and done separate as smaller type.<br><br>But it wd. be much nicer to do none of these things.<br><br>yours<br>C.S. Lewis",
        type: "Letter",
        notes: null
