        issueOrVolume: "vol. 39",
        pageRange: "43",
        year: 2023,
        monthAndDay: null,
        textProvided: "29 March 1960<br><br>Dear Shepherd<br><br>Yes, I see the point, <i>Pearsall on the Flour</i> might some day have the same kind and degree of value as <i>Macrobius on Sispioun</i>. My protest, however, was not against notes that lead the student back into the whole culture presupposed in the poem, but to the nullification of parallels between Middle English poems, as if, whenever a character in Wodehouse had a cup of tea some future Macrobius recorded in the places where characters in J. Austen, H. James, D.H. Lawrence, W.W. Jacob, and Marie Corelli did the same. And I wasn’t afraid the reader wd. <i>resent</i> it; only that they wd. acquire a habit of not looking at the notes at all. But of course any attempt to draw the “Thus far and no farther” line must be arbitrary.<br><br>I go for a lightning trip to Greece on Sunday so I shan’t be able to deal with AL for some time.<br><br>Yours<br>C.S. Lewis",
